  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Fridel crafts Alkylation of Benzene takes place when ALkyl halide reacted with Benzene in presence of Anhydrous&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AlCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>​.&nbsp;</p><p>Now Alkenes can form Alkyl Benzene in Industrial process. The process involves the reaction of Benzene and Ethylene in presence of an Anhydrous&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AlCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>catalyst promoted by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math>.</p><p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math> on reaction produce ethyl benzene as follows</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0008_e67101381eca.png\" style=\"width: 565px; height: 189px;\" /></p>"
